1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Today we live in an age of mechanical marvels, but if we look back 2000 years, we may be surprised by what we find.

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Fantastic machines were in common use in the ancient world, from war machines to automatic doors and even robotic theaters.

3
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
The ancients even had a steam engine.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now we reveal how the first age of machines was in fact in the ancient world.

5
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now on Modern Marvel's Ancient Discoveries 3.

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
We have for centuries marveled at the world of the ancient Greeks.

7
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:04,000
A world where the human mind was set free to look beyond the everyday drudgery of life

8
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:09,000
and think of greater things.

9
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:20,000
A time of science, art and philosophy, dazzling new ideas that they would export to the rest of the world.

10
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:24,000
And at the heart of this age of wonder stood a great city.

11
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Not in Greece or Italy, but here on the coast of ancient Egypt, in a place called Alexandria.

12
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
While the Romans thought of war and the Greeks thought of philosophy, in Alexandria they thought of thought itself.

13
00:01:45,001 --> 00:01:52,000
These halls once echoed to the footsteps of Plato, Aristotle and Archimedes.

14
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:58,000
But you might also have found another less well-known face here.

15
00:01:58,001 --> 00:02:02,000
He was not a philosopher or poet, but an inventor,

16
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:07,000
and he almost started an industrial revolution 2,000 years ago.

17
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:11,000
They called him Mekinos, the machine man.

18
00:02:11,001 --> 00:02:14,000
But we know him as Heron of Alexandria.

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Two thousand years before the modern day,

20
00:02:20,001 --> 00:02:23,000
Heron was building his own machine age

21
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:27,000
and in the process creating devices that still amaze us today.

22
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Heron lived in Alexandria at a time when it was the most cosmopolitan city on Earth.

23
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
This beautiful city, once home to the legendary Queen Cleopatra,

24
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:42,000
was the brains behind the classical world.

25
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Great minds from across the world were drawn here to trade

26
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:49,000
in the most valuable of commodities,

27
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:53,000
not gold or slaves or corn, but knowledge.

28
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
The great library was the bank for this knowledge.

29
00:02:59,001 --> 00:03:03,000
Here were gathered together all the writings of the Greeks,

30
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:05,000
Romans, Egyptians, and more.

31
00:03:05,001 --> 00:03:08,000
Halls piled to the ceiling with scrolls of knowledge,

32
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:12,180
with scrolls of mathematics, philosophy, geography, and history,

33
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:17,200
all vying for space with plays, fables, songs, and poems.

34
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:20,200
Today, hardly any of these works survive,

35
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:24,700
and we can only dimly imagine what wonders once lay in Alexandria.

36
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:27,700
But we do know that hidden in its vaults

37
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:32,460
lay the plans for the most extraordinary machines ever created.

38
00:03:32,461 --> 00:03:42,640
the great library at alexandria was i think the intellectual powerhouse of the ancient world it

39
00:03:42,641 --> 00:03:47,220
was the largest and the best library in the mediterranean if you were looking for any

40
00:03:47,221 --> 00:03:51,820
particular book there was always a good chance that you could read it at alexandria

41
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:59,620
attached to the library at alexandria was an institution called the museum

42
00:03:59,621 --> 00:04:06,000
Not a museum as we would imagine it, with objects on display in glass cases and cabinets.

43
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:10,900
Museum was a place to muse, to think, to discuss, to invent.

44
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,560
Many of the professors in the museum had written the greatest books in the library.

45
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:19,800
Some devoted their energies to figuring out how the world worked.

46
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:24,340
And some spent their time planning, devising and building inventions.

47
00:04:24,341 --> 00:04:31,340
It was here in the museum that Heron set about creating his extraordinary inventions.

48
00:04:31,341 --> 00:04:38,340
Today, none of his machines survive, and we know almost nothing about the man himself.

49
00:04:38,341 --> 00:04:45,340
But we do know about his marvelous machines, because Heron wrote books on how to build them,

50
00:04:45,341 --> 00:04:50,340
and incredibly, copies of some of those books survive.

51
00:04:54,340 --> 00:05:08,340
They open a window on the wonderful mechanized world he knew.

52
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:14,360
Stored in one of the modern world's great repositories of knowledge, Oxford University,

53
00:05:14,361 --> 00:05:18,620
it is still possible to find a copy of one of Heron's books.

54
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:25,620
It is a rare document which gives a tantalizing glimpse of surprising ancient machines.

55
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:40,500
Although the original is long lost, this 16th century copy can take us straight back into

56
00:05:40,501 --> 00:05:51,260
heron's world.

57
00:05:51,261 --> 00:05:56,760
Within these pages, this master mechanic described his inventions and the sort of places you

58
00:05:56,761 --> 00:06:08,380
might find his robotic devices.

59
00:06:08,381 --> 00:06:13,420
Some were for the home, some simply there as entertainment for dinner parties.

60
00:06:13,421 --> 00:06:19,060
Others whirred and clanked away in theaters, producing amazing special effects.

61
00:06:19,061 --> 00:06:27,140
But the most likely place to find one of Harren's machines wasn't in either of those places.

62
00:06:27,141 --> 00:06:35,720
It was in the temples.

63
00:06:35,721 --> 00:06:42,200
Nowhere in the ancient world was more cosmopolitan than Alexandria, so nowhere had more religions

64
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:45,800
all vying for attention.

65
00:06:45,801 --> 00:06:51,260
Today Islam is the city's main religion, but 2,000 years ago there were many, many more

66
00:06:51,261 --> 00:06:53,140
faiths to choose from.

67
00:06:53,141 --> 00:06:58,680
We have to think about in the ancient world that there is stiff competition between different

68
00:06:58,681 --> 00:07:06,620
cults, Roman cults, Greek cults, Egyptian cults, cults for political leaders, and all

69
00:07:06,621 --> 00:07:08,998
sorts of mixes between these.

70
00:07:08,999 --> 00:07:14,000
So if you had something special that would attract attention,

71
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:20,400
then that would give you an edge in the market to attract visitors to your temple.

72
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:26,269
In other words, what priests of all these religions needed was magic.

73
00:07:26,270 --> 00:07:27,340
And that's just

74
00:07:27,341 --> 00:07:33,240
what Heron could provide with fantastic machines which would appear to move as if commanded

75
00:07:33,241 --> 00:07:34,178
by the gods.

76
00:07:34,179 --> 00:07:40,040
He would even create automatic temple doors which would open and close when

77
00:07:40,041 --> 00:07:42,565
a sacrifice was given to the gods.

78
00:07:42,566 --> 00:07:46,500
Heron was using his talents as a magician and showman

79
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:51,880
to trick the ancient worshippers into believing they had witnessed miracles happening in front

80
00:07:51,881 --> 00:07:53,120
of their very eyes.

81
00:07:54,940 --> 00:07:57,720
Heron is credited with inventing the steam engine

82
00:07:57,721 --> 00:07:59,580
during the first century A.D.,

83
00:07:59,581 --> 00:08:01,580
but the Greeks only used it as a novelty.

84
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:04,120
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

85
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:15,240
One of Heron's most ingenious machines

86
00:08:15,241 --> 00:08:17,540
was his automatic temple doors.

87
00:08:17,541 --> 00:08:20,200
The idea was simple but brilliant.

88
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:24,140
As a priest and congregation approached their temple,

89
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:27,880
the doors would magically open to welcome them inside.

90
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:30,880
In front of the huge closed doors of the temple,

91
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:35,840
the priest would theatrically step forward and light a fire on an altar by the doors.

92
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:40,820
Beneath this altar, an amazing array of pipes, containers, and weights

93
00:08:40,821 --> 00:08:45,340
would then swing into play, unbeknown to the expectant congregation.

94
00:08:45,341 --> 00:08:48,820
water flowed unseen into buckets

95
00:08:48,821 --> 00:08:51,120
that counterweighted the huge doors

96
00:08:51,121 --> 00:08:58,940
above ground the priest might now make a final offering

97
00:08:58,941 --> 00:09:00,980
on the fire as the heat drove

98
00:09:00,981 --> 00:09:02,460
the hidden mechanism below

99
00:09:02,461 --> 00:09:10,680
then suddenly as the counterweights filled

100
00:09:10,681 --> 00:09:12,900
the doors slowly creaked open

101
00:09:12,901 --> 00:09:15,860
as if the gods were pleased with the sacrifice.

102
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:26,520
A fantastic finale was provided by the sounding of a fanfare

103
00:09:26,521 --> 00:09:29,600
as the mechanism blew compressed air through a trumpet.

104
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:32,440
To Heron, it was simply mechanics,

105
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:35,640
but to the congregation, it was a miracle.

106
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:56,100
There could have been few better ways to impress your audience.

107
00:09:56,101 --> 00:10:00,640
And there is evidence that even the great temple at Ephesus in Turkey, one of the wonders

108
00:10:00,641 --> 00:10:04,740
of the ancient world, may have been fitted with these automatic doors.

109
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:25,040
Steve Poole, a kinetic artist, is fascinated with the work of Heron and has built a scale

110
00:10:25,041 --> 00:10:28,160
model to show how the automatic doors actually worked.

111
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:31,560
A cylinder of air is heated using fire.

112
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:38,200
The air inside expands and travels through a tube into a container of water, in this

113
00:10:38,201 --> 00:10:39,460
case a red canister.

114
00:10:39,461 --> 00:10:46,460
The hot air pushes the water out of the canister and into another container which gradually fills.

115
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:59,460
The mechanism uses a counterbalance and when one container fills and drops another rises, causing the doors to swing open.

116
00:10:59,461 --> 00:11:08,520
open.

117
00:11:08,521 --> 00:11:12,480
It is a simple machine, but an extremely effective one,

118
00:11:12,481 --> 00:11:15,640
and one which shows Heron had mastered the elemental forces

119
00:11:15,641 --> 00:11:19,100
of water, fire, and air.

120
00:11:19,101 --> 00:11:21,280
When the altar fire was extinguished,

121
00:11:21,281 --> 00:11:24,440
the air cooled and drew water back out of the container,

122
00:11:24,441 --> 00:11:26,840
so it rose and closed the doors.

123
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:43,460
Heron invented many temple machines which included these automated moving figures, which made offerings to the temple fire when it was lit.

124
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:04,300
Of course, it was one thing to persuade people to come into your temple, but quite another

125
00:12:04,301 --> 00:12:09,140
to persuade them to part with their money.

126
00:12:09,141 --> 00:12:13,080
And money was the key to running a successful religion.

127
00:12:13,081 --> 00:12:17,520
How to get people to spend it and how to collect it were just two of the problems Heron also

128
00:12:17,521 --> 00:12:24,660
had answers for.

129
00:12:24,661 --> 00:12:30,720
This is the first, a holy water dispenser.

130
00:12:30,721 --> 00:12:38,100
It was the world's first coin-operated slot machine.

131
00:12:38,101 --> 00:12:52,101
As people entered the temples, they were required to wash their hands using holy water that

132
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:58,980
had been blessed by the priests.

133
00:12:58,981 --> 00:13:03,140
Blessing and selling holy water was time-consuming for the priests.

134
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:06,900
So Heron invented a machine that made their lives easier

135
00:13:06,901 --> 00:13:11,740
and was more efficient.

136
00:13:11,741 --> 00:13:14,780
When a worshipper placed a drachma coin in the top,

137
00:13:14,781 --> 00:13:18,780
out came a precisely measured cupful of expensive, but holy water.

138
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:30,060
For the worshippers, it once more looked like a miracle.

139
00:13:30,061 --> 00:13:35,120
In reality, the dropping of the coin caused a lever to fall, opening a valve to let out

140
00:13:35,121 --> 00:13:36,120
water.

141
00:13:53,840 --> 00:14:00,880
For those who could see the inside, it was simply another mechanical marvel.

142
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:04,580
But for the eager audience, it was pure magic.

143
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:12,260
In solving the priest's problem of dispensing holy water,

144
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:16,100
Heron had invented the coin-operated vending machine

145
00:14:16,101 --> 00:14:20,500
1,800 years before its modern equivalent was first patented.

146
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:31,300
Other miraculous machines may also have helped to bring in the worshippers, such as this

147
00:14:31,301 --> 00:14:38,860
replica in a major exhibition of ancient technology in Athens.

148
00:14:38,861 --> 00:14:47,220
This magical Greek device is a smart tap.

149
00:14:47,221 --> 00:14:53,460
As water is taken out with a cup, it automatically fills itself back to the right level.

150
00:14:53,461 --> 00:14:58,080
This type of simple miracle machine may well have provided the inspiration for Harren's

151
00:14:58,081 --> 00:15:12,081
water dispenser.

152
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:18,300
It is hard to imagine today that these silent temple ruins may once have been filled with

153
00:15:18,301 --> 00:15:25,300
such magical machinery, performing what looked like miracles to an awestruck audience.

154
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:39,600
These were huge temples, some of the wealthiest and the most splendid in the ancient world.

155
00:15:39,601 --> 00:15:44,680
Heron's automatic inventions would have added further to the sheer sense of splendor and

156
00:15:44,681 --> 00:15:47,320
or that any worshipper would have experienced.

157
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:54,260
But Heron had one more money-making device for the temple priests,

158
00:15:54,740 --> 00:15:58,700
one which would allow worshippers to think they were talking with the gods,

159
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:02,520
but which was in fact much more dishonest.

160
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:12,280
This is the omen machine, the world's first great con job.

161
00:16:12,281 --> 00:16:18,400
Heron's most famous mathematical work is Proposition 1.8,

162
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:22,600
which calculates the area of a triangle based on the lengths of its sides.

163
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:25,320
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

164
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:37,440
In the ancient world, one of the reasons for going to a temple

165
00:16:37,441 --> 00:16:39,980
was to find out what the future might hold.

166
00:16:39,981 --> 00:16:44,220
and ancient peoples were happy to pay for a glimpse of things to come.

167
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:48,540
For this they would consult Heron of Alexandria's omen machine.

168
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:58,120
Someone coming into the temple which was equipped with one of these devices

169
00:16:58,121 --> 00:17:00,660
would not, of course, see all the workings.

170
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:03,300
As they walked into the gloom of the interior,

171
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:06,940
they would see just this wheel and a mechanical bird.

172
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:15,740
After paying a suitable fee, they could ask the god a simple yes or no question.

173
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:21,580
Turn the wheel and the bird would either sing or not.

174
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:23,180
The god had spoken.

175
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:31,140
But of course it was not the gods who had made this bird sing, but Haran.

176
00:17:31,141 --> 00:17:43,400
inside the mechanism a series of cogs ropes and pulleys made the bird sing when the wheel was

177
00:17:43,401 --> 00:17:49,440
turned a simple cog attached to the inside of the device could then be disengaged perhaps

178
00:17:49,441 --> 00:17:53,700
by a priest to silence the bird if the other answer was required

179
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:07,460
Singing gave one answer, not singing the other.

180
00:18:07,461 --> 00:18:10,600
The priest could choose which was most appropriate.

181
00:18:10,601 --> 00:18:20,960
It is the oldest mechanical confidence trick in the world.

182
00:18:20,961 --> 00:18:23,200
And the wonderful bird song?

183
00:18:23,201 --> 00:18:29,140
Just a cup attached to a warbling whistle being lowered into a tank of water.

184
00:18:29,141 --> 00:18:43,141
Sound was the key to many of Heron's inventions, from his omen machines to his elaborate windmill-powered musical organ.

185
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:51,840
But getting it all to work together is much harder than it might seem.

186
00:18:51,841 --> 00:19:00,760
Richard Windley is an artist and musical instrument maker

187
00:19:00,761 --> 00:19:03,320
who has been inspired by Heron's machines

188
00:19:03,321 --> 00:19:06,720
which incorporated sound movement within their design

189
00:19:06,721 --> 00:19:12,200
Richard recreated a mechanical singing bird

190
00:19:12,201 --> 00:19:15,260
similar to the ones that once adorned the omen machine

191
00:19:15,261 --> 00:19:20,940
it was an intriguing challenge to see if he could make Heron's bird sing

192
00:19:20,941 --> 00:19:30,860
the drawings of heron which i discovered and the writings which are sort of fairly descriptive but

193
00:19:30,861 --> 00:19:35,740
rather tantalizing give the basic outlines of how he managed to get these things working

194
00:19:35,741 --> 00:19:43,020
this machine uses the siphon mechanism the same concept which flushes modern toilets

195
00:19:43,021 --> 00:19:49,580
water is poured into the top and when it reaches a certain point the siphon action sucks out water

196
00:19:49,581 --> 00:19:50,506
at high speed.

197
00:19:50,507 --> 00:19:55,400
This fast-flowing water pushes the air in the pipes and forces it upwards.

198
00:19:55,920 --> 00:20:02,160
It causes a warbling whistle to sing and opens and closes a valve, making the bird's beak move.

199
00:20:09,220 --> 00:20:13,940
This is a very simple version with just a single bird, but if we talk about sort of whole flocks

200
00:20:13,941 --> 00:20:17,620
of these things, perhaps six or eight or ten of these things all being driven by water pressure,

201
00:20:17,621 --> 00:20:21,900
then, you know, the effect would be staggering,

202
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:26,660
particularly to probably a public that were not technologically knowledgeable.

203
00:20:47,583 --> 00:20:50,900
And Heron built far more elaborate machines than this.

204
00:20:50,901 --> 00:20:57,560
In this strange device, when you lift an apple from a pedestal,

205
00:20:57,880 --> 00:21:01,620
a figure of Hercules fires an arrow from a bow toward a dragon.

206
00:21:08,020 --> 00:21:11,860
One time when Heron's omen machines were in particular demand

207
00:21:11,861 --> 00:21:15,500
was during war, a frequent event in the ancient world.

208
00:21:15,501 --> 00:21:22,500
Everyone wanted to ask the gods if they or their loved ones would survive.

209
00:21:22,501 --> 00:21:26,500
But Harren could help with more than just omen machines.

210
00:21:26,501 --> 00:21:34,500
In Alexandria, he had read treatises by Archimedes, Tecebius and Dionysius on building war machines.

211
00:21:34,501 --> 00:21:39,500
Perhaps he could devise machines that would not just predict the outcome of wars,

212
00:21:39,501 --> 00:21:42,500
but would actually help to win them.

213
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:47,500
Heron turned his prodigious mechanical mind to the art of warfare.

214
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:54,500
Heron had automated doors, fountains, and musical boxes.

215
00:21:54,501 --> 00:21:57,500
Now he wanted to automate war.

216
00:21:57,501 --> 00:22:00,500
Building on the theoretical work of the great Greek mathematicians,

217
00:22:00,501 --> 00:22:03,500
he described automatic war machines,

218
00:22:03,501 --> 00:22:07,500
stronger, faster, and more fearless than the foot soldiers of the day.

219
00:22:07,501 --> 00:22:11,500
A deadly military technology centuries ahead of its time.

220
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:25,500
Alan Wilkins has been using ancient descriptions of war machines to try to rebuild these fearsome

221
00:22:25,501 --> 00:22:30,960
devices. This is a Greek catapult, but it is no ordinary weapon.

222
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:41,300
This is the Polybolos, the multi-shooter. It's the first machine gun. And it's over

223
00:22:41,301 --> 00:22:48,300
over 2,000 years before what is now regarded as the first machine gun, the Victorian Gatling gun.

224
00:22:54,660 --> 00:22:57,480
This ingenious weapon had many unique features.

225
00:22:57,481 --> 00:23:00,420
It was one of the first uses of a chain mechanism

226
00:23:00,421 --> 00:23:01,756
anywhere on earth.

227
00:23:01,757 --> 00:23:07,100
It could fire a bolt further and harder than any man, and most terribly,

228
00:23:07,101 --> 00:23:12,060
could fire bolt after relentless bolt without rest.

229
00:23:12,061 --> 00:23:14,240
The Roman army in particular had an interest

230
00:23:14,241 --> 00:23:16,380
in such lethal devices.

231
00:23:16,381 --> 00:23:18,960
With a huge border to defend, such weapons

232
00:23:18,961 --> 00:23:20,660
offered them a distinct advantage.

233
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:26,640
From the Roman perspective, men like Heron

234
00:23:26,641 --> 00:23:28,820
could help to hold the empire together.

235
00:23:37,100 --> 00:23:43,100
Here on Trajan's column in Rome can be seen an automatic Cerro Ballista, or hand catapult.

236
00:23:47,100 --> 00:23:51,100
This machine originated in Alexandria and dates from Heron's day.

237
00:23:51,101 --> 00:23:54,100
It may even have been devised by him.

238
00:23:54,101 --> 00:23:57,100
Alan Wilkins has built a working model.

239
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:05,100
The device mimics the action of a traditional archer firing arrows, but with much more force.

240
00:24:07,100 --> 00:24:10,100
It is a primitive military robot.

241
00:24:16,100 --> 00:24:21,100
The main length of the machine represents the outstretched arm of the archer.

242
00:24:21,101 --> 00:24:35,101
A double-handed cranking system then draws the string back, clamped between two metal

243
00:24:39,400 --> 00:24:40,720
hooks.

244
00:24:40,721 --> 00:24:45,960
When the arm is fully stretched, these steel fingers then release, driving the bolt forward

245
00:24:45,961 --> 00:24:50,540
with terrific force.

246
00:24:50,541 --> 00:24:53,840
Alexandria, of course, received a tremendous boost.

247
00:24:53,841 --> 00:24:57,520
By Heron's day, it was, of course, a Roman province.

248
00:24:57,940 --> 00:25:01,340
And the Romans obviously backed this research there.

249
00:25:01,420 --> 00:25:04,620
They continued to want to use engineers like Heron,

250
00:25:05,320 --> 00:25:06,900
because they knew they were the best.

251
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:14,040
Many devastating war machines would have been used in battle,

252
00:25:14,460 --> 00:25:17,860
but others were developed as prototypes for future use.

253
00:25:17,861 --> 00:25:22,840
Here are these Alexandrian engineers thinking up all these ideas

254
00:25:22,841 --> 00:25:27,140
which are a couple of thousand years before they can ever be realized.

255
00:25:27,940 --> 00:25:32,180
They didn't allow the limitations of their own technology to limit their ideas.

256
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:45,700
But Heron had more to offer the Alexandrians

257
00:25:45,701 --> 00:25:48,580
than just siege engines and miracle machines.

258
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:55,940
He wanted to entertain them.

259
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:59,960
And to do that, he chose a place where anything could happen.

260
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:02,720
The theater.

261
00:26:07,340 --> 00:26:10,860
Heron describes almost 100 machines and toys

262
00:26:10,861 --> 00:26:13,200
in his surviving work, Pneumatica,

263
00:26:13,201 --> 00:26:15,780
including a fire engine and a wind organ.

264
00:26:16,340 --> 00:26:18,100
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

265
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:29,460
It was in the indulgent world of the theatrical arena

266
00:26:29,461 --> 00:26:32,980
that Hera knew his genius at creating magical machines

267
00:26:32,981 --> 00:26:34,620
could truly be recognized.

268
00:26:36,380 --> 00:26:40,960
In ancient times, theater was at the center of many people's lives.

269
00:26:40,961 --> 00:26:45,960
It was here that people could join together and discover new ideas and experiences.

270
00:26:47,960 --> 00:26:56,960
Theaters such as this vast one at Epidaurus still stand as a testament to their enduring popularity with the people of the classical world.

271
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:04,960
This was as close as the ancients got to cinema, their very own field of dreams.

272
00:27:10,960 --> 00:27:19,140
Even the acoustics were precisely scientifically designed like a modern cinema's to heighten the audience's experience.

273
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:38,120
For the classical world theater was of huge importance and had been for centuries.

274
00:27:38,121 --> 00:27:44,120
The audiences flocked in huge numbers to the theatre, which in a sense was the form of mass communication of the day.

275
00:27:44,121 --> 00:27:50,120
There there were elements of politics, culture, even religion played out before a mass audience.

276
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:56,120
Of course it was hugely attractive to Heron because he had a ready-made audience,

277
00:27:56,121 --> 00:28:00,120
an audience which was conditioned and habituated almost, one might say,

278
00:28:00,121 --> 00:28:03,120
to going to the theatre and enjoying spectacle entertainments there.

279
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:15,120
Heron knew he could add to this experience.

280
00:28:15,121 --> 00:28:19,120
The skills he had learned building war machines and temple illusions

281
00:28:19,121 --> 00:28:24,120
could finally be given free reign on a stage where anything was possible.

282
00:28:24,121 --> 00:28:33,120
He began by devising theatrical staging with the eerie ability to move on and off stage on its own.

283
00:28:33,121 --> 00:28:40,120
This is the first mechanical marvel Heron used to astound the theater audiences of ancient

284
00:28:40,121 --> 00:28:45,194
Alexandria. It was built using Heron's original descriptions.

285
00:28:45,195 --> 00:28:47,440
It demonstrates how gravity

286
00:28:47,441 --> 00:28:54,120
can be used to make a piece of scenery move on and off stage, apparently of its own will.

287
00:28:54,121 --> 00:28:59,280
It is the precursor of the modern computerized scenery we see in the West End of London and

288
00:28:59,281 --> 00:29:03,000
New York's Broadway today.

289
00:29:03,001 --> 00:29:05,000
He wrote in his books,

290
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:07,240
machines and automata,

291
00:29:07,320 --> 00:29:10,120
which is the most powerful technology of his era.

292
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:13,400
He wrote closed automata systems,

293
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:14,920
systems with an entrance,

294
00:29:15,480 --> 00:29:20,280
and automata theaters that have all the theatrical scenes.

295
00:29:29,240 --> 00:29:32,440
A compartment in the top of the device contains sand,

296
00:29:32,441 --> 00:29:36,300
and when it was released through a series of holes, it pulled down a weight.

297
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:40,040
The weight was attached to a rope which was wound around an axle.

298
00:29:40,700 --> 00:29:43,780
When the rope unwound from the axle, the device moved forward.

299
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:49,200
When fully unwound, a lever clicked, the weight was lifted, and the device trundled back again.

300
00:29:54,280 --> 00:29:57,960
Heron wrote a whole treatise about automatic theatres.

301
00:29:57,961 --> 00:30:02,960
The idea of the automatic theatre is that it can move by itself.

302
00:30:03,620 --> 00:30:06,500
Automaton in Greek actually means a self-mover.

303
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:11,400
Heron is quite interested in making absolutely sure

304
00:30:11,401 --> 00:30:15,560
that you make it quite small so that the audience won't suspect

305
00:30:15,561 --> 00:30:19,060
that there's actually a person inside the theatre running the show.

306
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:37,420
A simple spindle with pegs which made all these movements possible was in fact one of

307
00:30:37,421 --> 00:30:40,960
Heron's greatest inventions.

308
00:30:40,961 --> 00:30:45,900
This carefully wound arrangement of pegs and ropes is what a modern computer scientist

309
00:30:45,901 --> 00:30:53,800
would call a program.

310
00:30:53,801 --> 00:30:59,400
No doubt the audiences at Alexandria's theaters marveled at these moving sets for a while.

311
00:30:59,401 --> 00:31:04,980
But as Heron knew, audiences are always looking for something new to grab their attention.

312
00:31:04,981 --> 00:31:10,920
He had automated the set, but what about the actors?

313
00:31:10,921 --> 00:31:16,740
This would be his next bold step, to create an entirely automatic theater production with

314
00:31:16,741 --> 00:31:23,740
The automatic sets, actors and effects that would run on its own for over 20 minutes.

315
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:34,640
Heron decided to automate the classic Greek tale of tragedy and bloody revenge, Nopolis.

316
00:31:34,641 --> 00:31:41,640
The story tells of how King Nopolis seeks revenge after his son is killed by Ajax at the close of the Trojan Wars.

317
00:31:46,740 --> 00:31:53,740
The play begins with 12 characters repairing a warship, all moving automatically.

318
00:31:53,741 --> 00:32:02,740
Below the action, the mechanics all remain hidden inside the box.

319
00:32:02,741 --> 00:32:10,740
Leaving the audience as astounded and intrigued to see all these wooden characters moving in unison.

320
00:32:10,741 --> 00:32:13,540
to see all these wooden characters moving in unison.

321
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:18,580
To complete the illusion,

322
00:32:18,980 --> 00:32:21,220
Heron added sounds and special effects.

323
00:32:28,180 --> 00:32:31,320
Scenes changed and back cloths dropped automatically,

324
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:34,980
providing a new background for each piece of the action in turn.

325
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:38,640
To prevent collisions between characters,

326
00:32:38,641 --> 00:32:41,420
the action all took place on different planes,

327
00:32:41,700 --> 00:32:44,800
which rose and fell at different times during the performance.

328
00:32:50,400 --> 00:32:54,180
Powering the whole pageant was a system of weights and ropes,

329
00:32:54,600 --> 00:32:56,400
sand glasses and seed hoppers,

330
00:32:56,920 --> 00:32:59,540
utilizing gravity to provide the required energy.

331
00:33:04,560 --> 00:33:08,320
The complexity of so many choreographed automatic elements

332
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:12,000
was an incredible feat of engineering and ingenuity.

333
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:19,720
As the ship in the story encountered a storm,

334
00:33:20,660 --> 00:33:24,860
Herron used an automatic thunder machine to shake some fear into the audience.

335
00:33:38,320 --> 00:33:52,320
The goddess Athena appears on cue to command the weather as the story reaches its climax.

336
00:33:53,220 --> 00:33:57,900
She causes a bolt of lightning to hit Ajax, the enemy in the story.

337
00:33:58,500 --> 00:34:02,340
He dies, and our hero Nopolis finally has his revenge.

338
00:34:08,320 --> 00:34:19,420
As the final sounds rang out around the theater,

339
00:34:19,920 --> 00:34:21,780
the first time this play was performed,

340
00:34:21,781 --> 00:34:26,220
the audience must have been enraptured by the spectacle in front of them.

341
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:52,320
Actually making the play happen was no less miraculous than the event the audience had just witnessed.

342
00:34:56,620 --> 00:35:01,440
It might not be magic, but it required a mathematical genius to make it work.

343
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:04,840
Timing every element of the story correctly,

344
00:35:05,440 --> 00:35:08,960
calculating the exact weights for balances and counterbalances,

345
00:35:09,540 --> 00:35:12,080
the speed of the cogs and the order of the scenes

346
00:35:12,081 --> 00:35:14,480
would baffle many engineers today

347
00:35:14,481 --> 00:35:17,480
and was perhaps miraculous in itself.

348
00:35:23,480 --> 00:35:26,800
The engine which drove the whole show was this hopper.

349
00:35:27,720 --> 00:35:29,600
It harnessed the power of gravity

350
00:35:29,601 --> 00:35:32,360
and was filled with either sand or seeds.

351
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:36,500
The slow release of these then started a chain of events.

352
00:35:37,540 --> 00:35:40,600
As the seeds fell, a weight on top of them lowered.

353
00:35:41,220 --> 00:35:44,840
That weight then pulled a rope, which then in turn turned a spindle.

354
00:35:45,460 --> 00:35:47,960
This spindle was effectively the master control.

355
00:35:48,760 --> 00:35:53,540
It held the program stored in a complex system of ropes wound around pegs,

356
00:35:53,860 --> 00:35:58,700
which set off different aspects of the show when the rope was unwound past certain points.

357
00:35:58,701 --> 00:36:06,560
It triggered the individual pieces of the theater, such as this spindle, which revolved to make dolphins apparently leap through the waves.

358
00:36:18,040 --> 00:36:28,340
In Alexandrian theaters like this, crowds may once have gasped as roars of thunder and flashes of lightning emerged from the strange automatic theater on stage.

359
00:36:28,700 --> 00:36:30,860
and reverberated around the auditorium.

360
00:36:32,980 --> 00:36:36,200
The theatre in Alexandria, I think, was at the centre of the city's life.

361
00:36:37,160 --> 00:36:41,480
Also, and importantly, before the highly critical audience in the theatre in Alexandria,

362
00:36:41,860 --> 00:36:44,000
reputations were won and lost.

363
00:36:44,001 --> 00:36:46,740
And it's in that highly competitive context

364
00:36:46,741 --> 00:36:50,900
that we should see Heron of Alexandria's spectacular stage devices.

365
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:54,120
These were devices meant to wow the audience.

366
00:36:54,480 --> 00:36:57,280
It's difficult to recapture something of their impact,

367
00:36:57,281 --> 00:37:03,180
but I imagine that it was like watching the first talking movies at the beginning of the 20th century.

368
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:06,360
Something that would make you want to come back to the theater,

369
00:37:06,700 --> 00:37:10,300
something that would make you remember Heron of Alexandria as an inventor.

370
00:37:11,940 --> 00:37:15,240
Heron was an entertainer and a flamboyant inventor.

371
00:37:15,960 --> 00:37:20,280
While many of Heron's designs were intended to either impress worshippers in a temple

372
00:37:20,281 --> 00:37:22,840
or to entertain audiences in a theater,

373
00:37:22,841 --> 00:37:28,180
Some were mechanical toys or novelties built simply for amusement in the home.

374
00:37:28,900 --> 00:37:31,840
But one invention could have changed the world forever.

375
00:37:32,900 --> 00:37:39,380
It's a steam-powered engine, invented by Heron thousands of years before its time.

376
00:37:41,580 --> 00:37:45,500
Heron's work Catatrica deals with his study of light and mirrors.

377
00:37:46,100 --> 00:37:49,640
In it, he states that vision results from light rays emitted by the eyes,

378
00:37:49,641 --> 00:37:52,220
and that these rays travel with infinite speed.

379
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:54,660
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

380
00:38:05,880 --> 00:38:09,920
Heron had discovered the power of fire, water, and air.

381
00:38:10,360 --> 00:38:14,140
And finally, he combined his knowledge to create steam power.

382
00:38:14,141 --> 00:38:28,141
This would lead to the invention of a simple but remarkable device.

383
00:38:28,740 --> 00:38:35,340
The strange machine is Heron's Steam Ball, the first steam turbine engine and the fastest

384
00:38:35,341 --> 00:38:43,940
revolving mechanical instrument in the ancient world.

385
00:38:43,941 --> 00:38:47,940
Aaron was just half a step away from inventing the steam engine.

386
00:38:47,941 --> 00:38:50,940
He knew the principles of steam power.

387
00:38:50,941 --> 00:38:53,940
He had designed a revolutionary device.

388
00:38:53,941 --> 00:38:58,940
What might have happened if he'd taken that extra half step?

389
00:39:13,940 --> 00:39:23,860
I'm heating up this container here so that it generates steam.

390
00:39:23,861 --> 00:39:28,940
There's water in here and the steam will come up through these pipes into this ball and

391
00:39:28,941 --> 00:39:31,940
out through these jets and then it should spin.

392
00:39:43,940 --> 00:39:46,100
It was a sight to behold.

393
00:39:57,860 --> 00:40:01,080
Heron had managed to control the power of steam.

394
00:40:01,740 --> 00:40:03,280
It was a remarkable breakthrough.

395
00:40:13,940 --> 00:40:19,940
This device could perhaps have started an industrial revolution 2,000 years ago.

396
00:40:28,940 --> 00:40:33,940
Had Herron combined what he knew about pistons with this steam ball invention,

397
00:40:33,941 --> 00:40:36,940
he might have made a true steam engine.

398
00:40:36,941 --> 00:40:49,420
The invention of the steam engine in 1721 ushered in the Industrial Revolution, bringing in

399
00:40:49,421 --> 00:40:55,480
its wake mass production, high-speed travel, and the birth of the age of the consumer.

400
00:40:55,481 --> 00:40:58,960
The modern world was forged by the steam engine.

401
00:40:58,961 --> 00:41:04,180
But how would the world look today if that giant leap had been taken by Heron nearly

402
00:41:04,181 --> 00:41:11,180
2,000 years earlier.

403
00:41:13,740 --> 00:41:20,740
So why didn't Heron's greatest invention start a revolution?

404
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:25,940
Perhaps he simply didn't make the connections and never saw this wonderful machine as anything

405
00:41:25,941 --> 00:41:31,120
more than a toy.

406
00:41:31,121 --> 00:41:36,680
or more ominously in a world run on cheap and plentiful slave power

407
00:41:36,681 --> 00:41:43,680
perhaps no one saw the need for labor saving devices

408
00:41:48,680 --> 00:41:51,980
it's fascinating to speculate how the world might be different today

409
00:41:51,981 --> 00:41:56,240
if we hadn't forgotten heron of alexandria and his wonderful inventions

410
00:41:56,241 --> 00:41:59,060
if they'd been embraced rather than rejected by

411
00:41:59,061 --> 00:42:00,367
medieval scholars.

412
00:42:00,368 --> 00:42:05,740
If the people who'd seen the wondrous steam ball had thought of it as more

413
00:42:05,741 --> 00:42:09,186
than an idle curiosity or just a children's plaything.

414
00:42:09,187 --> 00:42:11,100
After all, it's clear from his

415
00:42:11,101 --> 00:42:16,940
inventions that Heron of Alexandria knew about steam mechanics and knew the basis of computer

416
00:42:16,941 --> 00:42:18,465
programming.

417
00:42:18,466 --> 00:42:28,760
There's an interesting scholarly question, which is, you look at Heron's writings

418
00:42:28,761 --> 00:42:34,320
and those of his contemporaries, and we feel they're almost approaching technological lift-off.

419
00:42:34,720 --> 00:42:39,840
That in another generation or two, with the steam engine and other inventions in the wake of that,

420
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:46,780
we might almost have been facing an industrial revolution taking place 2,000 years before it actually occurred.

421
00:42:52,860 --> 00:42:56,860
We consider ourselves today at the cutting edge of technology,

422
00:42:56,861 --> 00:43:01,860
but in many ways we are only just beginning to catch up with developments in the ancient world.

423
00:43:07,860 --> 00:43:13,860
Two thousand years ago, there was a world more familiar and more advanced than we could have imagined.

424
00:43:13,861 --> 00:43:20,860
imagined.

425
00:43:22,240 --> 00:43:29,240
A time when mechanical machines brought wonder and magic to ancient cities.

426
00:43:30,860 --> 00:43:35,140
A time that has been forgotten for centuries and is only now beginning to

427
00:43:35,141 --> 00:43:37,140
be revealed again.

428
00:43:37,141 --> 00:43:39,960
What ancient discoveries still await us?

429
00:43:39,961 --> 00:43:42,940
And what others have been lost forever?

430
00:43:42,941 --> 00:43:46,480
Heron has given us just a glimpse of his amazing world.

431
00:43:51,480 --> 00:43:54,480
But what could our world have been like today

432
00:43:54,481 --> 00:43:58,000
if we had not forgotten Heron of Alexandria?

433
00:44:04,860 --> 00:44:06,520
Tomorrow on the History Channel.

434
00:44:06,880 --> 00:44:11,500
Way back in the day, historians came up with a list of superhuman achievements,

435
00:44:11,501 --> 00:44:13,940
the must-sees of the ancient world.

436
00:44:14,420 --> 00:44:17,560
The pyramid at Giza is the largest and oldest of all.

437
00:44:17,780 --> 00:44:19,120
Can you name the other six?

438
00:44:19,940 --> 00:44:21,560
The Seven Wonders of the World,

439
00:44:21,960 --> 00:44:23,940
tomorrow at 8 on the History Channel.

